// Chronicle AP Euro: Dylan's Khare + Harris Euro Notes, parsed act by act (Units 2-5).
// NOTES[act] = [[section heading, [[indent (-1 = bold line), text], ...]], ...]; NCHAR[act] = [[name, what they did], ...] from each Main Characters list.
const NOTES = {"2.1": [["Setting", [[0, "1400s–1700s"], [0, "European states look for new trade routes, wealth, land, and access to Asian goods."], [0, "Better navigation and ships make long-distance voyages more practical."], [0, "Exploration leads to conquest → colonies → Atlantic trade → competition between European states."]]], ["Main Characters", [[0, "Prince Henry the Navigator → encourages Portuguese exploration"], [0, "Bartolomeu Dias → reaches southern tip of Africa"], [0, "Vasco da Gama → reaches India by sailing around Africa"], [0, "Christopher Columbus → sails west for Spain and reaches the Americas"], [0, "Amerigo Vespucci → argues these lands are a separate continent"], [0, "Ferdinand Magellan → expedition completes first circumnavigation"], [0, "Hernán Cortés → conquers Aztec Empire"], [0, "Francisco Pizarro → conquers Inca Empire"], [0, "Bartolomé de las Casas → criticizes Spanish treatment of Indigenous peoples"]]], ["Portuguese and Spanish Exploration", [[0, "Portugal explores down the African coast looking for a direct sea route to Asian trade."], [0, "Dias rounds the southern tip of Africa."], [0, "Da Gama reaches India, giving Portugal direct access to Indian Ocean trade."], [0, "Spain backs Columbus, who reaches the Americas in 1492 while trying to reach Asia."], [0, "European exploration quickly becomes conquest and colonization."]]], ["Conquest of the Americas", [[0, "Cortés defeats the Aztec Empire; Pizarro defeats the Inca Empire."], [0, "Europeans benefit from:"], [1, "superior weapons and military technology"], [1, "alliances with Indigenous enemies of the Aztecs/Incas"], [1, "devastating diseases such as smallpox"], [0, "Disease causes enormous Indigenous population decline and makes conquest much easier."]]], ["Spanish Colonial System", [[0, "Spain builds an organized colonial empire designed to produce wealth."], [0, "Encomienda gives colonists access to Indigenous labor."], [0, "Repartimiento/mita later organizes forced labor through government systems."], [0, "Large estates called haciendas develop."], [0, "Mining, especially silver, becomes extremely important."], [0, "Spain creates colonial governments and officials to maintain royal control."], [0, "Later Bourbon reforms attempt to tighten administration and increase revenue."]]], ["Las Casas and the Black Legend", [[0, "Bartolomé de las Casas condemns Spanish brutality toward Indigenous peoples."], [0, "His writings contribute to the Black Legend, the image promoted by Spain’s rivals that Spanish colonialism was uniquely cruel."], [0, "European rivals use these accusations partly to criticize and compete with Spain."]]], ["Atlantic Slavery", [[0, "As plantations and colonial economies grow, Europeans increasingly use enslaved Africans."], [0, "The Atlantic slave trade becomes a major part of the colonial economy."], [0, "Enslaved labor is especially important on plantations producing valuable crops."], [0, "This connects Europe, Africa, and the Americas in an expanding Atlantic economic system."]]], ["Columbian Exchange", [[0, "The Columbian Exchange transfers plants, animals, diseases, and people between the Old and New Worlds."], [0, "Europe receives foods and resources from the Americas."], [0, "Europeans introduce animals, crops, and diseases to the Americas."], [0, "Disease is one of the most destructive parts of the exchange for Indigenous populations."], [0, "The exchange permanently links European and American economies and societies."]]], ["Mercantilism & Rivalry", [[0, "European governments increasingly follow mercantilism:"], [1, "wealth is limited and states compete for it"], [1, "colonies supply raw materials"], [1, "colonies provide markets for finished goods"], [1, "governments try to export more and strengthen national wealth"], [0, "Overseas empires therefore become part of competition between Spain, Portugal, England, France, and the Dutch."], [0, "Exploration that begins as a search for trade routes becomes a struggle for global power."]]], ["Main Chain to Remember", [[-1, "New trade routes → exploration → conquest → colonies → forced labor/slavery → Columbian Exchange → Atlantic economy → mercantilism → European rivalry"]]]], "2.2": [["Setting", [[0, "Mid-to-late 1500s"], [0, "The Protestant Reformation has divided western Europe."], [0, "The Peace of Augsburg (1555) recognizes Catholicism and Lutheranism in the Holy Roman Empire but not Calvinism."], [0, "Religious disagreement becomes mixed with political struggles over monarchy, local rights, and state power."]]], ["Main Characters", [[0, "Philip II → Catholic king of Spain"], [0, "William of Orange → leader of Dutch resistance"], [0, "Elizabeth I → Protestant queen of England"], [0, "Mary, Queen of Scots → Catholic rival/threat to Elizabeth"], [0, "Catherine de Médicis → tries to preserve French monarchy during religious conflict"], [0, "Guise family → militant French Catholics"], [0, "Bourbon family → associated with Huguenots"], [0, "Admiral Coligny → important Huguenot leader"], [0, "Henry of Navarre / Henry IV → Huguenot Bourbon who eventually becomes king"]]], ["Story 1: Spain and the Dutch Revolt", [[0, "Charles V fails to restore religious unity and divides his possessions."], [0, "His son Philip II receives Spain, the Netherlands, and overseas possessions."], [0, "Philip is strongly Catholic and wants greater religious and political control."], [0, "The Netherlands is wealthy, values local privileges, and has growing Calvinism."], [0, "Conflict develops over:"], [1, "religion"], [1, "taxation"], [1, "Spanish centralization"], [1, "local political rights"], [0, "William of Orange becomes a major resistance leader."], [0, "Philip sends the Duke of Alba, whose harsh repression and Council of Blood increase resistance."], [0, "Dutch rebels known as the Sea Beggars help keep the revolt alive."], [0, "The Spanish Fury strengthens anti-Spanish feeling."], [0, "The Netherlands eventually divides:"], [1, "Catholic south moves back toward Spain"], [1, "Protestant north forms the Union of Utrecht"], [0, "The northern provinces effectively become independent, although Spain formally recognizes Dutch independence only in 1648."]]], ["England and Spain", [[0, "Elizabeth I establishes a Protestant settlement in England."], [0, "Catholic Mary, Queen of Scots becomes a threat because Catholics could view her as an alternative monarch."], [0, "After the Babington Plot, Mary is executed in 1587."], [0, "England supports the Dutch rebels and sailors such as Drake attack Spanish interests."], [0, "1588: Spanish Armada fails to defeat England."], [0, "This damages Philip II’s attempt to dominate Protestant opponents."]]], ["Story 2: French Wars of Religion", [[0, "Henry II dies in 1559, leaving a weak monarchy."], [0, "France becomes divided between:"], [1, "Guise family → militant Catholics"], [1, "Bourbons/Huguenots → French Protestants and their allies"], [0, "Catherine de Médicis tries to balance factions and preserve royal authority."], [0, "1562: massacre at Vassy helps trigger open religious war."], [0, "France experiences repeated civil wars between Catholics and Huguenots."]]], ["St. Bartholomew’s Day Massacre", [[0, "Huguenot leader Admiral Coligny gains influence over King Charles IX."], [0, "An attempt is made on Coligny’s life."], [0, "Violence explodes in 1572 during the St. Bartholomew’s Day Massacre."], [0, "Thousands of Huguenots are killed."], [0, "The massacre strengthens Huguenot resistance and arguments that rulers who persecute true religion can be resisted."]]], ["Henry of Navarre and the End of the Wars", [[0, "By the 1580s, France faces a three-way struggle between:"], [1, "Henry III"], [1, "the Catholic Guise faction"], [1, "Henry of Navarre"], [0, "Henry III has Guise leaders killed but is himself assassinated in 1589."], [0, "With no direct male heir, Henry of Navarre is the legal successor and becomes Henry IV."], [0, "He is originally Protestant but converts to Catholicism in 1593 to gain broader acceptance."], [0, "1598: Edict of Nantes"], [1, "France remains Catholic"], [1, "Huguenots receive limited religious protections"], [0, "Henry IV behaves as a politique, putting political stability ahead of strict religious uniformity."]]], ["Main Idea", [[-1, "The religious wars begin as serious Catholic-Protestant conflicts, but rulers increasingly realize that destroying the state over religion is politically disastrous."]]], ["Main Chain to Remember", [[-1, "Reformation divides Europe → Philip II fights Dutch Protestants → Dutch revolt succeeds → French Catholics and Huguenots fight → St. Bartholomew’s massacre → Henry IV becomes king → converts → Edict of Nantes → political stability begins outweighing religious purity"]]]], "2.3": [["Setting", [[0, "Holy Roman Empire, 1618–1648"], [0, "Catholics, Lutherans, and Calvinists are still divided"], [0, "Peace of Augsburg recognized Catholics and Lutherans, but not Calvinists"], [0, "The empire is split into hundreds of states"], [0, "Many German princes also fear the emperor becoming too powerful"]]], ["Main Characters", [[-1, "Ferdinand II"], [0, "Catholic Habsburg emperor"], [0, "wants stronger Catholic control"], [0, "helps trigger the war in Bohemia"], [-1, "Frederick V"], [0, "Calvinist ruler"], [0, "chosen by rebellious Bohemians"], [-1, "Maximilian of Bavaria"], [0, "important Catholic ally"], [0, "supports Ferdinand"], [-1, "Christian IV of Denmark"], [0, "Protestant king"], [0, "enters to help the Protestant side"], [-1, "Wallenstein"], [0, "major commander for Ferdinand"], [0, "becomes extremely powerful"], [-1, "Gustavus Adolphus"], [0, "Protestant king of Sweden"], [0, "strong military leader"], [-1, "Cardinal Richelieu"], [0, "Catholic French leader"], [0, "wants to weaken the Habsburgs"]]], ["Phase 1: Bohemian Revolt", [[-1, "1618: Defenestration of Prague"], [0, "Ferdinand limits Protestant freedoms in Bohemia"], [0, "Protestants rebel and throw his officials out a window"], [0, "Bohemians choose Frederick V as king"], [-1, "1620: Battle of White Mountain"], [0, "Ferdinand defeats Frederick"], [0, "Bohemia returns to Habsburg/Catholic control"]]], ["Phase 2: Denmark Enters", [[-1, "1625"], [0, "Christian IV of Denmark enters for the Protestant side"], [0, "also wants more influence in Germany"], [0, "Ferdinand uses Wallenstein"], [0, "Denmark is defeated"], [-1, "1629: Edict of Restitution"], [0, "Ferdinand orders many Protestant-held Church lands returned to Catholics"], [0, "Calvinism still not recognized"], [0, "Protestants become even more worried"]]], ["Phase 3: Sweden Enters", [[-1, "1630"], [0, "Gustavus Adolphus of Sweden enters"], [0, "helps the Protestant side"], [0, "Sweden also wants more political influence"], [-1, "1632: Battle of Lützen"], [0, "Protestants win"], [0, "Gustavus Adolphus is killed"], [-1, "1634"], [0, "Ferdinand has Wallenstein assassinated because he becomes too independent and powerful"]]], ["Phase 4: France Enters", [[-1, "1635"], [0, "Catholic France enters against the Catholic Habsburgs"], [0, "Richelieu wants to weaken Habsburg power"], [-1, "This is the major turning point:"], [-1, "religion matters less, political power matters more"]]], ["Destruction", [[0, "Most fighting happens in German lands"], [0, "towns and farms are destroyed"], [0, "armies loot and spread disease"], [0, "textbook estimates roughly one-third of the German population dies"]]], ["1648: Peace of Westphalia", [[0, "ends the war"], [0, "Calvinism officially recognized"], [0, "German princes gain more control"], [0, "Holy Roman Empire stays divided"], [0, "Dutch and Swiss independence recognized"], [0, "France and Sweden gain influence"]]], ["Main Chain to Remember", [[-1, "Peace of Augsburg leaves problems → Ferdinand II restricts Protestants → Defenestration of Prague → Frederick V rebels → White Mountain → Denmark enters → Sweden enters → France enters → war becomes more political → Peace of Westphalia"]]]], "2.4": [["Setting", [[0, "Late 1600s into early 1700s"], [0, "After the religious wars, states focus more on how political power should be organized."], [0, "Three models:"], [1, "Dutch Republic → wealthy republic"], [1, "England → monarchy limited by Parliament"], [1, "France → monarchy moves toward absolutism"]]], ["Main Characters", [[0, "William III of Orange → Dutch leader; later king of England"], [0, "James I → believes in divine right; clashes with Parliament"], [0, "Charles I → pushes royal power further and triggers civil war"], [0, "Oliver Cromwell → Parliament’s military leader; later Lord Protector"], [0, "Charles II → restores the monarchy"], [0, "James II → Catholic king removed in Glorious Revolution"], [0, "Richelieu & Mazarin → strengthen French monarchy"], [0, "Louis XIV → begins personal rule and pushes absolutism"]]], ["Story 1: The Dutch Republic", [[0, "The United Provinces remain a republic rather than a monarchy."], [0, "Provinces keep strong local power while the States General handles shared affairs."], [0, "Economy thrives through trade, shipping, banking, and the Dutch East India Company."], [0, "William III of Orange becomes important when France threatens the Netherlands."], [0, "By the 1700s, Dutch power declines as Britain overtakes it in naval and commercial strength."], [0, "Main idea: a republic can become rich and powerful without an absolute king."]]], ["Story 2: England — King vs. Parliament", [[0, "Becomes king in 1603 after Elizabeth I."], [0, "Believes in the divine right of kings."], [0, "Clashes with Parliament over money, taxation, and royal authority."], [0, "Puritans want the Church of England more Protestant."], [0, "This creates the conflict that Charles I makes much worse."], [0, "Continues pushing royal authority and fighting Parliament over money."], [0, "1628: Petition of Right → challenges taxation without Parliament’s consent and arbitrary imprisonment."], [0, "Charles later rules without Parliament during his Personal Rule."], [0, "Religious conflict with Scotland forces him to call Parliament back in 1640."], [0, "The central issue becomes who really controls England: king or Parliament?"], [0, "1642: war begins."], [0, "Cavaliers = king; Roundheads = Parliament."], [0, "Oliver Cromwell becomes a major Parliament commander."], [0, "Parliament wins; Charles I is executed in 1649."], [0, "England becomes a republic, but Cromwell dominates government."], [0, "Rules as Lord Protector and relies heavily on military power."], [0, "After his death in 1658, the republic weakens."], [0, "1660: Charles II restores the monarchy."], [0, "Parliament remains important."], [0, "His Catholic sympathies and ties with Louis XIV create suspicion."], [0, "James II becomes king in 1685 and openly favors Catholics."], [0, "Parliament fears Catholic absolutism."], [0, "1688: opponents invite William III and Mary to England."], [0, "James flees → Glorious Revolution."], [0, "1689 Bill of Rights limits royal power and strengthens Parliament."], [0, "This largely settles the king-vs-Parliament fight in Parliament’s favor."], [0, "England becomes a constitutional/parliamentary monarchy."], [0, "Robert Walpole later shows power increasingly shifting toward Parliament and ministers."]]], ["Story 3: France Moves the Opposite Way", [[0, "Richelieu and Mazarin strengthen royal authority and weaken opposition."], [0, "1649–1652: Fronde → nobles rebel against royal centralization but fail."], [0, "The Fronde teaches Louis XIV that powerful nobles can threaten the monarchy."], [0, "1661: Mazarin dies → Louis begins personal rule with no chief minister."], [0, "France moves opposite England:"], [1, "England limits the king"], [1, "France strengthens the king"], [0, "This sets up Louis XIV’s absolutism in Act 5."]]], ["Main Chain to Remember", [[-1, "Dutch Republic grows rich through trade → James I creates tension with Parliament → Charles I pushes royal power too far → Civil War → Charles I executed → Cromwell → monarchy restored → James II sparks another crisis → Glorious Revolution → Parliament gains lasting power → France moves toward Louis XIV’s absolutism."], [-1, "Character count: ~4,289, so this stays right on your 4,300 target."]]]], "2.5": [["Setting", [[0, "Mid-1600s to early 1700s"], [0, "France moves toward absolute monarchy while England is limiting its king."], [0, "Richelieu and Mazarin already strengthened royal authority."], [0, "Fronde (1649–1652) = noble rebellions that fail and teach young Louis XIV that powerful nobles can threaten the crown."], [0, "1661: Mazarin dies; Louis begins personal rule with no chief minister."]]], ["Main Characters", [[0, "Louis XIV → “Sun King”; makes monarchy dominant in war, religion, and the economy"], [0, "Richelieu & Mazarin → strengthen royal government before Louis"], [0, "Bossuet → defends divine right of kings"], [0, "Colbert → helps strengthen royal finances/economy"], [0, "William III of Orange → major opponent of Louis’s expansion"], [0, "Philip of Anjou / Philip V → Louis’s grandson who becomes king of Spain"], [0, "John Locke → argues for government by consent, opposite of absolutism"]]], ["Louis Builds Absolutism", [[0, "Louis does not control every local detail. Nobles, local institutions, and parlements still exist."], [0, "But the monarchy dominates major state matters, especially war, religion, and the economy."], [0, "Versailles becomes a political tool as well as a palace."], [0, "Important nobles spend time there competing for royal favor, offices, and status, making them more dependent on Louis and less able to organize against him."], [0, "Bossuet argues rulers are appointed by God and ultimately answer to God."], [0, "Colbert helps support the monarchy through stronger finances and economic policy."]]], ["Expansion and Balance of Power", [[0, "Louis wants stronger, safer French borders and repeatedly goes to war."], [0, "War of Devolution (1667–1668): Louis claims land in the Spanish Netherlands through his wife, Marie Thérèse."], [0, "England, Sweden, and the Dutch form the Triple Alliance to stop further French expansion."], [0, "1672: Louis attacks the Dutch Republic; William III becomes one of his main enemies."], [0, "Peace of Nijmegen (1678–1679) gives France more territory, including Franche-Comté."], [0, "Pattern: as France grows stronger, other states form coalitions to stop it. This is the balance of power."], [0, "Later, the League of Augsburg / Nine Years’ War forms another coalition against Louis."], [0, "Peace of Ryswick (1697) checks further French expansion."]]], ["Religious Uniformity", [[0, "Louis believes political unity works best with religious unity."], [0, "He suppresses Jansenists, a strict Catholic movement connected with Cornelius Jansen and Port-Royal."], [0, "The bigger issue is the Huguenots, French Protestants."], [0, "1685: Louis revokes the Edict of Nantes."], [0, "Protestant churches and schools close; ministers are forced to convert or leave."], [0, "About a quarter million Huguenots flee France, many skilled workers and merchants."], [0, "This increases religious uniformity but hurts France economically and damages Louis’s reputation."]]], ["War of the Spanish Succession", [[0, "1700: Charles II of Spain dies without a direct heir."], [0, "He leaves the throne to Louis’s grandson Philip of Anjou, who becomes Philip V."], [0, "Other powers fear France and Spain could become too closely united under the Bourbon family."], [0, "England, Holland, and the Holy Roman Emperor form the Grand Alliance."], [0, "War lasts 1701–1714."], [0, "Utrecht (1713) and Rastatt (1714) end the conflict."], [0, "Philip V keeps Spain, but France and Spain do not merge."], [0, "England gains Gibraltar and Minorca, and Louis recognizes the Hanoverian succession."]]], ["Absolutism vs. Limited Government", [[0, "Bossuet: king’s authority comes from God; absolute monarchy is legitimate."], [0, "John Locke: government should rest on consent and protect life, liberty, and property."], [0, "Main contrast:"], [1, "France → stronger king"], [1, "England → stronger Parliament"]]], ["End of Louis XIV", [[0, "1715: Louis XIV dies."], [0, "France remains a major European power."], [0, "But repeated wars and royal spending leave serious financial problems."], [0, "His reign shows both the strength and the cost of absolutism."]]], ["Main Chain to Remember", [[-1, "Richelieu/Mazarin strengthen monarchy → Fronde scares Louis → Louis begins personal rule in 1661 → Versailles + divine right strengthen royal authority → wars expand France → coalitions form to preserve balance of power → Louis represses Jansenists and Huguenots → Edict of Nantes revoked → Spanish Succession crisis → Louis dies in 1715 with France still powerful but financially strained."]]]], "2.6": [["Setting", [[0, "Late 1600s to early 1700s"], [0, "Central/eastern Europe is more agricultural and less economically developed than western Europe."], [0, "After the Thirty Years’ War, rulers try to build stronger centralized states."], [0, "Main story: Austria strengthens, Prussia becomes a military state, and Russia rises under Peter the Great."], [0, "Poland is the contrast because weak central authority prevents strong state-building."]]], ["Main Characters", [[0, "Leopold I → Habsburg ruler who resists the Ottomans"], [0, "Charles VI → creates the Pragmatic Sanction"], [0, "Maria Theresa → Charles VI’s daughter and Habsburg heir"], [0, "Frederick William, Great Elector → strengthens Brandenburg-Prussia"], [0, "Frederick William I → expands Prussia’s army"], [0, "Frederick II → invades Silesia in 1740"], [0, "Peter the Great → westernizes, centralizes, and expands Russia"]]], ["Poland: Weak Central Authority", [[0, "Poland has an elective monarchy and powerful nobles."], [0, "The Sejm is the noble legislature."], [0, "Liberum veto lets one member block action and even end a session."], [0, "This makes effective government difficult and leaves Poland open to foreign influence."]]], ["Austria and the Habsburgs", [[0, "After 1648, Habsburg emperors have less control over the Holy Roman Empire, so they focus on their hereditary lands."], [0, "Austria expands against the Ottoman Empire."], [0, "1683: Ottomans besiege Vienna; Polish king John III Sobieski helps save the city."], [0, "Habsburg victories bring more of Hungary under Austrian control."], [0, "The empire is diverse, so rulers still bargain with local nobles."], [0, "Charles VI has no son and creates the Pragmatic Sanction so Maria Theresa can inherit."], [0, "When Charles dies in 1740, Frederick II of Prussia invades Silesia, showing how fragile the agreement was."]]], ["Prussia and the Hohenzollerns", [[0, "Brandenburg-Prussia starts as scattered territories."], [0, "Frederick William, the Great Elector, strengthens central government, raises taxes, and builds a powerful army."], [0, "He works with the Junkers: they support the monarchy and dominate the army/government while keeping control over peasants."], [0, "The Great Elector welcomes French Protestant refugees after Louis XIV revokes the Edict of Nantes, hoping their skills will help the economy."], [0, "Frederick William I expands the army further; by 1740 Prussia has one of Europe’s largest armies despite its small population."], [0, "Frederick II inherits this military machine and uses it to seize Silesia."]]], ["Russia and Peter the Great", [[0, "The Romanov dynasty begins in 1613, restoring stability after the Time of Troubles."], [0, "Russia is still separated from much of western Europe."], [0, "Peter the Great decides Russia needs a stronger military and western methods."], [0, "In 1697, he travels through western Europe studying ships, weapons, and industry."], [0, "After the streltsy rebel, Peter crushes them and weakens old military opposition."], [0, "He forces western-style changes on the boyars, including shaving traditional beards and changing dress."], [0, "Peter builds a larger army through conscription and develops a navy."]]], ["Great Northern War and Peter’s Reforms", [[0, "1700–1721: Russia fights Sweden in the Great Northern War for Baltic access."], [0, "Sweden defeats Russia at Narva, but Peter rebuilds."], [0, "1709: Battle of Poltava → Russia decisively defeats Charles XII."], [0, "Peace of Nystad (1721) gives Russia Baltic territory and makes it a major European power."], [0, "Peter founds St. Petersburg in 1703, a western-facing capital and symbol of reform."], [0, "He reorganizes government into administrative colleges."], [0, "Table of Ranks (1722) ties status to service in the military or bureaucracy rather than only birth."], [0, "Peter puts the Russian Orthodox Church under tighter state control through the Holy Synod."], [0, "His son Aleksei, who opposed Peter’s direction, dies in custody in 1718 after being investigated for conspiracy."]]], ["Main Chain to Remember", [[-1, "Poland stays weak → Austria strengthens → Prussia builds a military state → Peter westernizes and militarizes Russia → Russia defeats Sweden and gains Baltic access → central/eastern Europe becomes a major arena of competing powers."], [-1, "Character count: ~4,289, right on your standard target."]]]], "3.1": [["Setting", [[0, "Late 1600s to late 1700s"], [0, "European powers compete for territory, trade, and overseas colonies."], [0, "Louis XIV's wars weaken France financially and encourage alliances against French expansion."], [0, "Spain's Bourbon rulers strengthen control over their American colonies."], [0, "Main story: France faces financial problems while colonial trade and African slavery expand."]]], ["Main Characters", [[0, "Louis XIV → French king whose wars strain France"], [0, "Philip V → Louis's grandson who inherits Spain"], [0, "Louis XV → becomes French king at age five"], [0, "Duke of Orléans → governs France as regent"], [0, "John Law → financial planner behind the Mississippi Bubble"], [0, "Cardinal Fleury → promotes stability under Louis XV"], [0, "Charles III → Spanish king who strengthens colonial administration"]]], ["Louis XIV's Later Wars", [[-1, "Nine Years' War (1689–1697)"], [0, "1681: Louis occupies Strasbourg, threatening neighboring states."], [0, "League of Augsburg: England, Spain, Holland, Sweden, and German states oppose French expansion."], [0, "Peace of Ryswick (1697) limits France's gains and protects Holland."], [0, "Main idea: European alliances increasingly defend the balance of power."], [-1, "War of the Spanish Succession (1701–1714)"], [0, "1700: Charles II of Spain dies without a direct heir."], [0, "His inheritance goes to Philip of Anjou (Philip V), Louis XIV's grandson."], [0, "Other powers fear France and Spain becoming too powerful together."], [0, "Grand Alliance (1701): England, Holland, and the Holy Roman Empire fight France."], [0, "English commander Duke of Marlborough wins important victories."], [0, "Religion also matters: Catholic Stuart claims threaten England's Protestant succession."], [0, "Utrecht (1713) and Rastatt (1714): Philip keeps Spain, but the French and Spanish crowns cannot unite."], [0, "Britain gains Gibraltar and Minorca; Spain loses European possessions."], [0, "Louis recognizes the Protestant Hanoverian succession in Britain."]]], ["France After Louis XIV", [[0, "1715: Louis XIV dies, leaving France powerful but financially strained."], [0, "Five-year-old Louis XV inherits; the Duke of Orléans becomes regent."], [0, "John Law creates a bank issuing paper money and the Mississippi Company, connected to Louisiana trade."], [0, "Investors speculate heavily, driving company shares upward."], [0, "1720: Mississippi Bubble collapses when the bank cannot exchange enough paper money for gold."], [0, "Financial panic weakens confidence in the monarchy."], [0, "Orléans restores powers to the Parlement of Paris, allowing nobles greater influence."], [0, "Cardinal Fleury (1726) promotes peace and financial stability."], [0, "Fleury also continues repression of Jansenists, showing religion remains connected to royal authority."]]], ["Spanish Bourbon Colonial Reforms", [[0, "Bourbon kings adopt French administrative methods to increase colonial revenue and control."], [0, "Spain tries to strengthen its trade monopoly and reduce smuggling."], [0, "Charles III opens additional Spanish ports to colonial trade."], [0, "1776: Creates the Viceroyalty of Río de la Plata to improve administration."], [0, "1786: Introduces intendants to strengthen taxation and royal government."], [0, "Peninsulares = Spanish-born people; creoles = American-born people of Spanish descent."], [0, "Favoring peninsulares for government positions increases creole resentment."], [0, "Result: Greater royal control eventually encourages colonial resistance."]]], ["Piracy and Atlantic Trade", [[0, "Spain's trade restrictions encourage smuggling, piracy, and privateering."], [0, "Privateers attack enemy ships with government permission."], [0, "Buccaneers raid ships and settlements, sometimes dividing profits among crews."], [0, "European competition makes Atlantic trade increasingly important and violent."]]], ["African Slavery and Sugar Plantations", [[0, "Disease and harsh treatment devastate Indigenous populations, increasing European demand for enslaved African labor."], [0, "European traders acquire captives through West African slave markets and conflicts."], [0, "Brazil and the West Indies become major sugar-producing plantation regions."], [0, "Sugar profits encourage expansion of the transatlantic slave trade."], [0, "Enslaved Africans face brutal labor, disease, and high mortality."], [0, "1725: About 90% of Jamaica's population is enslaved Black people."], [0, "Africans bring languages, religious beliefs, and traditions that shape American societies."]]], ["Main Chain to Remember", [[-1, "Louis expands → European alliances resist → Spanish succession crisis → France becomes financially strained → Mississippi Bubble → noble opposition increases."], [-1, "Bourbon reforms strengthen colonial control → creole resentment grows → Atlantic plantations expand → African slavery increases → American societies are transformed."]]]], "3.2": [["Setting", [[0, "1700s: Atlantic plantations produce European wealth through enslaved African labor."], [0, "Rivalries expand from Europe into North America, the Caribbean, and India."], [0, "Main story: slave-based trade expands → global wars reshape power → British victory creates debts and colonial resistance."]]], ["Main Characters", [[0, "Frederick II → Prussian king who takes Silesia."], [0, "Maria Theresa → preserves Austria."], [0, "William Pitt the Elder → directs Britain's global war strategy."], [0, "Robert Clive → British victor in India."]]], ["Triangular Trade and Sugar (283–285)", [[0, "Portuguese and Spanish traders lead early, followed by Dutch and then British slave traders."], [0, "Triangular trade: European goods → Africa; enslaved Africans → Americas; sugar and colonial products → Europe."], [0, "Northern colonies trade fish, lumber, and rum with the Caribbean, also profiting from slavery."], [0, "Sugar becomes cheaper and more common through enslaved labor on Caribbean plantations."], [0, "Sweetened tea, coffee, and chocolate boost demand for sugar."], [0, "Dangerous sugar mills and brutal plantation labor generate profits."]]], ["Slavery, Resistance, and Religion (286–290)", [[0, "European demand and African warfare expand captive trading for goods and weapons."], [0, "Roughly 12 million Africans are forcibly transported to the Americas."], [0, "Middle Passage: Deadly Atlantic voyage involving overcrowding, shackles, disease, and hunger."], [0, "Survivors undergo seasoning, forced adjustment to slavery and plantation labor."], [0, "Slave codes permit harsh punishments and make slavery hereditary."], [0, "Africans preserve cultures, build communities, and sometimes revolt."], [0, "Religion: Many convert to Catholicism or Protestantism; others preserve Islam or combine African beliefs with Christianity. Slaveholders use religion to encourage obedience, while later abolitionists oppose slavery on Christian grounds."], [0, "The 1789 Brookes slave-ship diagram exposes overcrowding and aids abolitionism."], [0, "Plantation slavery encourages racial prejudice and permanent, inherited enslavement."]]], ["Trade Rivalries and Austrian Succession (291–292)", [[0, "Wars combine European power politics and colonial trade rivalry."], [0, "War of Jenkins' Ear (1739): Britain fights Spain over colonial smuggling and Spanish searches of ships."], [0, "War of the Austrian Succession (1740–1748): Frederick II invades Silesia after Charles VI's death, challenging Maria Theresa."], [0, "With Hungarian support, Maria Theresa saves most Habsburg lands; France backs Prussia, Britain backs Austria."], [0, "Aix-la-Chapelle (1748): Prussia keeps Silesia, strengthening its rivalry with Austria."]]], ["Diplomatic Revolution and Seven Years' War (292–294)", [[0, "Diplomatic Revolution (1756): Old enemies France and Austria ally; Britain joins Prussia. Interests override old rivalries."], [0, "Seven Years' War (1756–1763): Frederick invades Saxony; Austria, France, Russia, and Sweden fight Prussia, supported by Britain."], [0, "Russian ruler Peter III withdraws in 1762, helping Frederick survive."], [0, "Hubertusburg (1763): Prussia keeps Silesia and becomes a European great power."], [0, "William Pitt focuses British resources on defeating France overseas."], [0, "Plassey (1757): Robert Clive defeats French-aligned forces in Bengal, increasing British influence in India."], [0, "Quebec (1759): Britain defeats France in Canada."], [0, "Treaty of Paris (1763): Britain gains Canada and French lands east of the Mississippi; France retains some sugar islands and Indian trading posts."], [0, "Britain gains global power, but European states face massive war debts."]]], ["Beginning of American Colonial Resistance (295)", [[0, "Britain tries to make colonists help fund the defense of its enlarged empire."], [0, "Sugar Act (1764): strengthens collection of import duties; Stamp Act (1765): taxes printed materials."], [0, "Colonists protest taxation without representation through the Stamp Act Congress, Sons of Liberty, and boycotts."], [0, "Britain repeals the Stamp Act in 1766, but the Declaratory Act insists Parliament may still legislate for the colonies."], [0, "Disputes deepen colonial resistance."]]], ["Main Chain to Remember", [[-1, "Sugar demand → plantation slavery expands → global trade rivalry → Austrian Succession and Seven Years' War → Britain wins territory but accumulates debt → taxes → colonial resistance."]]]], "3.3": [["Setting", [[0, "1500s–1600s: Europeans question explanations inherited from Aristotle, Ptolemy, and scholasticism."], [0, "Reformation and exploration also challenge traditional knowledge."], [0, "Main story: New astronomy → scientific methods → political theories → science/religion debates."]]], ["Main Characters", [[0, "Copernicus → sun-centered universe; Brahe → precise observations"], [0, "Kepler → elliptical orbits; Galileo → telescope evidence"], [0, "Newton → gravity; Bacon → empiricism; Descartes → deduction"], [0, "Hobbes → absolute government; Locke → natural rights"], [0, "Pascal → faith and limits of reason"]]], ["The Scientific Revolution (202–208)", [[0, "Ptolemy’s geocentrism: Earth-centered universe; epicycles explain irregular planetary movement."], [0, "Copernicus, On the Revolutions (1543): proposes heliocentrism (sun-centered universe). Still uses circles and epicycles, so it is not initially more accurate, but challenges tradition."], [0, "Brahe rejects full heliocentrism but gathers precise astronomical data."], [0, "Kepler, New Astronomy (1609): uses Brahe’s observations to prove planets follow elliptical orbits."], [0, "Galileo observes Jupiter’s moons, Venus’s phases, sunspots, and the moon’s uneven surface. His findings challenge perfect heavens and support Copernicus."], [0, "Galileo promotes mathematical laws and gains Medici patronage."], [0, "Newton, Principia (1687): laws of motion and universal gravitation explain movement on Earth and in the heavens through the same physical laws."]]], ["New Methods & Philosophy (208–211)", [[0, "Mechanism: Nature works like a predictable clock; some thinkers see God as its designer."], [0, "Bacon: empiricism = observation and experiments; induction = specific evidence → general conclusions."], [0, "Novum Organum (1620) challenges ancient authority; science should improve human life."], [0, "Descartes, Discourse on Method (1637): deduction = general principles → specific conclusions."], [0, "He doubts uncertain claims, trusts reason, and separates mind from body; God guarantees clear ideas."], [0, "Contrast: Bacon starts with evidence; Descartes starts with logic. Both challenge scholasticism."]]], ["Hobbes vs. Locke (212–215)", [[0, "Hobbes, Leviathan (1651): shaped by the chaos of the English Civil War and a mechanical view of humans."], [0, "Humans are selfish; without government, the state of nature is a violent “war of every man against every man.”"], [0, "A social contract gives a sovereign near-unlimited power to ensure peace. Hobbes values security over political freedom; the sovereign need not be a king."], [0, "Locke, Two Treatises (1690): opposes absolutism. People possess natural rights to life, liberty, and property."], [0, "Government exists by consent to protect rights; if rulers violate them, people can resist or replace rulers."], [0, "Letter Concerning Toleration (1689): favors toleration among many Christians, but excludes Catholics and atheists."], [0, "Essay Concerning Human Understanding (1690): mind begins as a blank slate, shaped by experience."], [0, "Contrast: Hobbes → order through absolute authority; Locke → rights protected by limited government."]]], ["Scientific Institutions & Women (215–218, 221)", [[0, "Universities adapt slowly; new societies share findings through experiments and debate."], [0, "Royal Society of London (1660) and French Academy of Science (1666) gain support from governments and patrons seeking useful knowledge."], [0, "Their discussions help prepare the Enlightenment."], [0, "Women face exclusion: Margaret Cavendish writes The Blazing World (1666); Émilie du Châtelet later translates Newton."]]], ["Science and Religious Faith (221, 224–226)", [[0, "Astronomy raises disputes over Scripture and Church authority, though many scientists remain religious."], [0, "Galileo (1615): nature and Scripture both reveal God; biblical passages about nature need not always be literal."], [0, "1616: Catholic authorities restrict teaching heliocentrism as fact."], [0, "1632–1633: Galileo publishes Dialogue, is condemned, forced to recant, and placed under house arrest."], [0, "Pascal, associated with Catholic Jansenism, stresses human weakness and divine grace."], [0, "In Pensées, reason cannot fully explain God; Pascal’s Wager says belief offers infinite possible gain."], [0, "Scientific advances coexist with superstition and witch hunts."]]], ["Main Chain to Remember", [[-1, "Ptolemy → Copernicus → Brahe/Kepler → Galileo → Newton → Bacon/Descartes → Hobbes vs. Locke → scientific societies → debates over faith."]]]], "3.4": [["Setting", [[0, "1700s: The Enlightenment applies scientific reasoning to government, religion, society, and the economy."], [0, "Inspired by Newton, Locke, British stability after 1688, and greater literacy."], [0, "Main story: Reason spreads through print → philosophes challenge institutions → religious toleration and reform gain support."]]], ["Main Characters", [[0, "Newton & Locke → intellectual foundations"], [0, "Voltaire → critic of intolerance and French authority"], [0, "Émilie du Châtelet → popularizes Newton’s science"], [0, "Immanuel Kant → independent reasoning"], [0, "John Toland → deism; David Hume → challenges miracles"], [0, "Gotthold Lessing → toleration; Lady Mary Wortley Montagu → challenges European prejudice"]]], ["Enlightenment Foundations (311–313)", [[0, "Philosophes believe reason can bring progress through education, commerce, and better government."], [0, "Newton: nature follows discoverable laws. Thinkers ask whether society can also be improved rationally."], [0, "Locke: natural rights, experience, and religious toleration influence Enlightenment thought."], [0, "Britain after the Glorious Revolution (1688) seems attractive: Parliament limits the monarch, trade thrives, and some toleration exists."], [0, "French censorship and noble privilege become targets of criticism."], [0, "Not all thinkers support equality; many accept class hierarchies or seek help from rulers."]]], ["Print Culture and Public Opinion (313–315)", [[0, "More literacy and cheaper books, newspapers, novels, journals, and pamphlets spread new ideas."], [0, "Coffeehouses, salons, libraries, and Masonic lodges create places for discussion."], [0, "Authors can earn money from publishing instead of depending only on wealthy patrons."], [0, "Expanding discussion creates public opinion, forcing governments to defend or explain their actions."], [0, "Coffeehouses are largely male spaces; their coffee and sugar are linked to Atlantic plantation slavery."]]], ["The Philosophes, Voltaire & Kant (315–318)", [[0, "Philosophes are writers and critics applying reason to politics, economics, society, and religion, not a united organization."], [0, "Many depend on wealthy patrons yet criticize censorship and inherited privilege."], [0, "Voltaire faces imprisonment for criticizing the powerful and admires England’s relative liberty."], [0, "Letters on the English (1733) praises English toleration and indirectly criticizes France; authorities condemn it."], [0, "With du Châtelet, he spreads Newton’s science. Candide (1759) mocks simplistic optimism about human suffering."], [0, "Kant, “What Is Enlightenment?” (1784): people should escape self-imposed immaturity by thinking for themselves, not blindly following authority."]]], ["Religion: Deism, Toleration & Criticism (318–320)", [[0, "Philosophes criticize Church wealth, censorship, religious persecution, and doctrines such as original sin and predestination that they believe discourage improvement."], [0, "They challenge religious institutions, not necessarily belief in God."], [0, "Deism: A rational God created a lawful universe but does not continually intervene; morality and nature matter more than dogma."], [0, "John Toland, Christianity Not Mysterious (1696): supports a rational understanding of religion."], [0, "Voltaire defends tolerance after Jean Calas, a Protestant wrongly convicted and executed. His Treatise on Toleration (1763) challenges fanaticism."], [0, "Lessing, Nathan the Wise (1779): advocates toleration across religions."], [0, "Lady Mary Wortley Montagu describes Ottoman society favorably and promotes smallpox inoculation learned there."], [0, "Hume (1748) questions miracle claims without convincing empirical evidence."], [0, "A small radical minority, including d’Holbach and La Mettrie, embraces atheism and materialism; most philosophes do not."], [0, "Limits remain: toleration is uneven and many Enlightenment writers accept social inequality."]]], ["Main Chain to Remember", [[-1, "Scientific Revolution → Newton/Locke inspire reform → print culture spreads ideas → public opinion grows → philosophes challenge authority → deism and toleration reshape religious debate → Enlightenment influences society and politics."]]]], "3.5": [["Setting", [[0, "Mid-to-late 1700s: philosophes move from criticizing religion to applying reason to law, the economy, and government."], [0, "France is the center of political debate because of costly wars, corruption, and a weak royal court."], [0, "Main story: limits of toleration → Jewish Enlightenment → Encyclopedia and practical reform → Montesquieu vs. Rousseau → critics of empire."]]], ["Main Characters", [[0, "Spinoza → secular Judaism; Mendelssohn → \"Jewish Socrates\""], [0, "Diderot & d'Alembert → Encyclopedia"], [0, "Beccaria → criminal law reform"], [0, "Quesnay & Dupont de Nemours → physiocrats"], [0, "Adam Smith → free markets"], [0, "Montesquieu → separation of powers"], [0, "Rousseau → general will"], [0, "Herder & Diderot → critics of empire"]]], ["Limits of Toleration (320-323)", [[0, "Kant, Religion within the Limits of Reason Alone (1793): most philosophes want to make religion a humane force, not abolish it."], [0, "Attacks on biblical miracles also undercut Hebrew scripture; some call Judaism primitive, further stigmatizing Jews."], [0, "Europeans learn about Islam mostly from hostile missionary, travel, and scholarly works (ex: Sale's English Qur'an, 1734)."], [0, "Islam used as a foil: Voltaire's Fanaticism, or Mohammed (1742) indirectly attacks Christian fanaticism; Montesquieu's Persian Letters (1721) uses Persian visitors to satirize Europe."], [0, "Montesquieu's Spirit of the Laws (1748) links Islam with despotism."]]], ["Jewish Enlightenment (323-324)", [[0, "Haskalah: Jewish thinkers use reason and science to renew their faith while engaging the secular world."], [0, "Spinoza (Netherlands): secularized Judaism; supports toleration but condemns Judaism itself."], [0, "Mendelssohn (Berlin): model for Lessing's Nathan the Wise; Jews can stay loyal to Judaism and to the state."], [0, "Jerusalem (1783): state should be religiously neutral, Jews deserve equal civil rights, and communities shouldn't excommunicate over opinions."], [0, "His toleration goes further than Locke's."]]], ["The Encyclopedia (324)", [[0, "Diderot & d'Alembert; first volume 1751, finished 1772 (17 text volumes + 11 of plates), 100+ authors."], [0, "A plea for free expression; survives censorship by hiding radical ideas in obscure articles or irony."], [0, "Practical articles on manufacturing, canals, ships, and farming."], [0, "Goal: secularize learning; focus on human well-being, not divine law; models from antiquity."], [0, "14,000-16,000 copies sold before 1789."]]], ["Law & Economics (325-327)", [[0, "Beccaria, On Crimes and Punishments (1764): attacks torture and capital punishment; wants speedy trials and certain punishment to deter crime."], [0, "Laws should bring the greatest good for the greatest number = utilitarianism; influences central/eastern European rulers."], [0, "Physiocrats (France): attack mercantilist regulation; government should protect property; agriculture is the basis of all wealth; favor bigger, more efficient farms."], [0, "Adam Smith, Wealth of Nations (1776): tariffs, monopolies, and navigation acts hold back growth; individuals pursuing self-interest grow the economy."], [0, "Rejects mercantilism's zero-sum idea; wealth can expand for everyone."], [0, "Founder of laissez-faire, but government still provides schools, armies, navies, roads, and risky ventures."], [0, "Four-stage theory: hunting/gathering → herding → farming → commercial; puts Europe on top and later justifies an imperial \"civilizing mission.\""]]], ["Montesquieu, Spirit of the Laws (328)", [[0, "Noble of the robe and parlement member."], [0, "Empirical: no single best government; depends on size, population, customs, economy, traditions, and climate."], [0, "For France: monarchy limited by intermediary bodies, especially aristocratic parlements → conservative means, reformist goal."], [0, "Separation of powers: king (executive), Parliament (legislative), courts (judicial) check each other."], [0, "Misreads Britain (misses patronage, corruption, cabinet system) but shapes later constitutional democracies."]]], ["Rousseau (328-331)", [[0, "Discourses (1750, 1755): civilization corrupts human nature; unequal property causes much evil; questions material progress."], [0, "Social Contract (1762): \"All men are born free, but everywhere they are in chains.\""], [0, "Community matters more than individuals; general will ≈ majority of informed voting citizens."], [0, "Obeying the general will = freedom; some must be \"forced to be free\" → radical direct democracy."], [0, "Wants a civic religion based on deism."], [0, "vs. Smith: Smith wants people prosperous; Rousseau wants them good, even if poor."], [0, "Little impact at the time, but influences French Revolution leaders."]]], ["Critics of Empire (331-333)", [[0, "Most philosophes support empire as spreading progress."], [0, "Diderot, Condorcet, Kant, and Herder condemn the conquest of the Americas, treatment of Native Americans, and African slavery."], [0, "Key ideas: shared humanity deserves respect; other cultures should be understood, not destroyed; cultures can't be ranked."], [0, "Herder: European culture isn't universal → cultural relativism."], [0, "Antislavery arguments spread; anti-empire arguments stay isolated until the late 1800s."]]], ["Women & Salons (333)", [[0, "Salon hostesses (Geoffrin, Lespinasse, Tencin) give philosophes contacts, status, and protection."], [0, "Madame de Pompadour helps overcome censorship of the Encyclopedia."], [0, "Philosophes want better education for women, not equality; Montesquieu says women aren't naturally inferior but keeps traditional views of marriage."]]], ["Main Chain to Remember", [[-1, "Toleration has limits → Mendelssohn pushes broader toleration → Encyclopedia spreads secular, practical knowledge → Beccaria, physiocrats, and Smith apply reason to law and economics → Montesquieu limits monarchy; Rousseau demands direct democracy → a few critics condemn empire → salons spread ideas, but women stay unequal."]]]], "3.6": [["Setting", [[0, "1763-1780s: Britain wins the Seven Years' War but is left with huge debt and a giant new North American empire."], [0, "Main story: Britain taxes colonies → colonists resist → independence → reform pressure in Britain → example for Europe."]]], ["Main Characters", [[0, "George III → wants more royal control over government"], [0, "Lord North → George III's chief minister (1770-1782)"], [0, "Thomas Paine → Common Sense"], [0, "John Wilkes → London radical; \"Wilkes and Liberty\""], [0, "William Pitt the Younger → young prime minister who sides with the king"]]], ["Britain's Problems After 1763 (295)", [[0, "Debt and domestic taxes are way up, so Britain wants the colonies (main winners of the war) to help pay."], [0, "New land from the St. Lawrence to the Mississippi needs an expensive military presence."]]], ["Taxes and Resistance (295-296)", [[0, "Sugar Act (1764): lower tax but strictly collected; smugglers tried without juries. Stamp Act (1765): taxes documents and newspapers."], [0, "Britain: legal and fair. Colonists: not represented in Parliament, so only their own assemblies can tax them."], [0, "Stamp Act Congress, Sons of Liberty, boycotts → repeal (1766), but Declaratory Act keeps Parliament's power to legislate for the colonies."], [0, "Pattern: tax → resist → repeal → try again, with tempers worse each time."], [0, "Townshend Acts (1767) → troops in Boston → Boston Massacre (1770)."], [0, "Tea Act (1773): cheaper tea but still taxed without consent → Boston Tea Party."]]], ["Road to Independence (296-297)", [[0, "Intolerable Acts (1774): close Boston's port, reorganize Massachusetts, quarter troops in homes, move officials' trials to England."], [0, "Quebec Act (1774): seen as blocking self-government west of the Appalachians."], [0, "Committees of correspondence → First Continental Congress (1774) → fighting at Lexington, Concord, Bunker Hill (1775)."], [0, "Common Sense (1776): only independence works; Britain drags America into European wars; \"absurd\" for an island to rule a continent."], [0, "Declaration of Independence (July 4, 1776) → France joins (1778) via Franklin; Spain joins (1779)."], [0, "Yorktown (1781) → Treaty of Paris (1783) recognizes independence."]]], ["American Political Ideas (297-298)", [[0, "Colonists see themselves defending English liberties from 1688, using Locke's Whig ideas for a popular revolution."], [0, "Commonwealthmen (Cato's Letters): patronage is corrupt and standing armies = tyranny. Ignored in Britain, but colonists believe them, and post-1763 policy seems to prove them right."]]], ["Events in Britain (298-299)", [[0, "George III picks his own ministers instead of the Whig families; critics call it tyranny, but he wants influence, not absolute power."], [0, "Wilkes attacks the government in The North Briton issue 45 (1763), is arrested and expelled, then refused his seat after winning reelection repeatedly; finally seated in 1774."], [0, "Colonists see the Wilkes affair as proof of a conspiracy against liberty."]]], ["Influence Goes Both Ways (299-300)", [[0, "\"No taxation without representation\" applies to many British subjects too."], [0, "Americans create a new way to found government: congresses and conventions based on consent of the governed (Stamp Act Congress 1765 → Constitutional Convention 1787)."], [0, "Yorkshire Association Movement (1778): property owners demand reform of corrupt elections; Parliament trims some royal power."], [0, "Pitt the Younger wins in 1784 with royal patronage, tries one reform in 1785, fails, drops it."], [0, "Result: George III regains influence, but loses the colonies."]]], ["Broader Impact (300-301)", [[0, "America proves government can work without kings or hereditary nobles, based on written constitutions and popular sovereignty."], [0, "Liberty still limited to free white men; women, slaves, and Native Americans excluded."], [0, "War debts push monarchies toward reform and conflict, setting up the French Revolution."]]], ["Main Chain to Remember", [[-1, "War debt → colonial taxes → resistance → Intolerable Acts → Continental Congresses → Common Sense → independence → France joins → Yorktown → reform pressure in Britain → government by consent inspires Europe."]]]], "4.1": [["Setting", [[0, "1770s-1789: France is a rich country with a broke government after the Seven Years' War and the American Revolution."], [0, "Main story: debt → failed tax reforms → nobles block the king → Estates General called → Third Estate takes over → National Assembly."]]], ["Main Characters", [[0, "Louis XVI → well-meaning but weak king who keeps backing down"], [0, "Marie Antoinette → Austrian queen, unpopular and attacked in pamphlets"], [0, "Maupeou → Louis XV's chancellor who abolishes the parlements"], [0, "Necker → finance director whose misleading 1781 report hides the debt"], [0, "Calonne → finance minister with a bold tax reform plan (1786)"], [0, "Brienne → replaces Calonne, finds the debt really is that bad"], [0, "Abbé Sieyès → \"What Is the Third Estate?\""]]], ["The Money Problem (354)", [[0, "By 1789, debt payments = just over half the royal budget."], [0, "The debt itself isn't huge; the problem is the government can't tax the nation's wealth."], [0, "Nobles and clergy are largely exempt, so peasants pay the most: taxes to the king, church, and local lords."], [0, "Bad harvest (1788) → bread prices soar → peasants face starvation."], [0, "Main point: only taxing nobles and the church can fix it."]]], ["King vs. Parlements (354-355)", [[0, "After Louis XIV, nobles try to win back power through the parlements (aristocratic courts)."], [0, "Maupeou abolishes the parlements; Louis XVI restores them in 1774, bringing back noble resistance."], [0, "Parlements use Enlightenment language (Montesquieu, physiocrats) to call the king despotic, and they gain public support."], [0, "Monarchy loses moral authority: Louis seen as detached; Marie Antoinette attacked for extravagance; royal family stays isolated at Versailles."], [0, "Contrast: Frederick II, Joseph II, and George III are seen as hardworking reformers; Louis isn't."]]], ["Failed Reforms (355-357)", [[0, "Necker's report (1781): claims a surplus by leaving out American war costs; reveals big pensions for court favorites. Makes new taxes harder to justify."], [0, "Calonne's plan (1786): lower some taxes (gabelle on salt), turn the corvée into money payments, free internal trade, and create a land tax everyone pays regardless of status."], [0, "Assembly of Notables (1787): nobles and clergy reject it, ask for Necker back, and say only the Estates General (last met 1614) can approve taxes."], [0, "Nobles' real goal: gain power at the king's expense and lead reform themselves."], [0, "Brienne tries a land tax; Parlement of Paris and the clergy refuse."], [0, "July 1788: Louis agrees to call the Estates General; Brienne resigns; Necker returns."]]], ["The Estates General (357-358)", [[0, "First Estate = clergy; Second = nobles; Third = everyone else, led by wealthy middle-class lawyers and professionals."], [0, "Sieyès: \"What is the Third Estate? Everything... What does it ask? To become something.\""], [0, "Big fight: vote by order (each estate gets 1 vote, so nobles + clergy win 2-1) or by head (each member votes)."], [0, "Parlement of Paris (Sept 1788) backs voting by order → Third Estate distrusts nobles."], [0, "Doubling the Third (Dec 1788): Third Estate gets twice as many deputies, so it wins if voting is by head."], [0, "Cahiers de doléances: lists of grievances against waste, unfair taxes, church corruption, and noble hunting rights; demand equality of rights. Even nobles' cahiers want reform."]]], ["National Assembly & Tennis Court Oath (358-360)", [[0, "May 1789: Estates General meets at Versailles, but voting method still undecided."], [0, "Third Estate refuses to meet separately → June 17: declares itself the National Assembly; clergy narrowly vote to join June 19."], [0, "Louis locks them out of their meeting room → June 20: Tennis Court Oath, swearing not to separate until France has a constitution."], [0, "June 27: Louis gives in and orders the clergy and nobles to join."], [0, "Renamed National Constituent Assembly; king can now only govern with it."]]], ["Main Chain to Remember", [[-1, "War debt → nobles and clergy dodge taxes → parlements block reform → Necker hides debt → Calonne's plan rejected → Estates General called → doubling the Third → fight over voting → National Assembly → Tennis Court Oath → Louis gives in."]]]], "4.2": [["Setting", [[0, "July 1789-1791: Paris crowds and peasants push the revolution past what the Assembly planned."], [0, "Hunger fuels everything: bad harvests (1787-88), a cold winter, and the highest bread prices since 1703."], [0, "Main story: Bastille → Great Fear → August 4 → Rights of Man → Women's March → Assembly rebuilds France → Church crisis → king tries to flee."]]], ["Main Characters", [[0, "Louis XVI → keeps resisting the revolution, badly"], [0, "Marie Antoinette → pushes the king to resist; helps plan the escape"], [0, "Necker → popular finance minister fired July 11"], [0, "Marquis de Lafayette → American Revolution hero; leads the National Guard"], [0, "Olympe de Gouges → Declaration of the Rights of Woman"], [0, "Julien Raymond → free man of color from St. Domingue (Haiti) demanding rights"], [0, "Pope Pius VI → condemns the revolution"], [0, "Count of Artois → king's brother; leading émigré"], [0, "Leopold II (Austria) & Frederick William II (Prussia) → Declaration of Pillnitz"]]], ["Fall of the Bastille (360-361)", [[0, "Louis gathers troops near Paris and fires Necker (July 11) → Parisians fear an attack on the Assembly."], [0, "Paris electors form a citizen militia and collect arms."], [0, "July 14, 1789: shopkeepers, artisans, and workers storm the Bastille for weapons; 98 killed; only 7 prisoners inside, none political."], [0, "Lafayette leads the National Guard; creates the red, white, and blue cockade → becomes the tricolor flag."], [0, "Louis visits Paris, wears the cockade, recognizes the city government and National Guard → admits he can't stop the revolution by force."], [0, "First journée: a day when Paris crowds change the revolution's direction."]]], ["Great Fear & August 4 (361-362)", [[0, "Great Fear (summer 1789): rumors that troops will seize peasant crops → peasants burn châteaux and feudal records, stop paying dues."], [0, "Night of August 4: nobles and clergy give up hunting/fishing rights, tithes, feudal dues, and tax exemptions."], [0, "Sale of offices abolished → careers open to talent, not birth."], [0, "Result: all citizens under the same laws; old social order effectively ends."], [0, "Assembly sees popular crowds as an ally against the king, but they'll demand a price later."]]], ["Declaration of the Rights of Man and Citizen (362-364)", [[0, "August 27, 1789; based on the Enlightenment and Virginia's Declaration of Rights (1776)."], [0, "Men \"born and remain free and equal in rights\": liberty, property, security, resistance to oppression."], [0, "Sovereignty comes from the nation; equality before the law; jobs by talent; due process; innocent until proven guilty; freedom of religion; taxes by ability to pay; property is sacred."], [0, "Two big universal ideas: civic equality + popular sovereignty → can spread beyond France."], [0, "Applies only to men; Rousseau-style thinking puts women in the home."]]], ["Women's March on Versailles (364-365)", [[0, "Louis stalls on approving the Declaration and the August 4 decrees; bread still scarce."], [0, "October 5, 1789: about 7,000 armed Parisian women march to Versailles demanding bread."], [0, "Louis agrees to the decrees; October 6 the royal family is forced to move to the Tuileries in Paris."], [0, "Assembly moves to Paris too; government now works under threat of the crowd."]]], ["Reconstruction of France (365-370)", [[0, "Goals: constitutional monarchy, free economy, rational administration, anticlericalism."], [0, "Constitution of 1791: one-house Legislative Assembly; king has only a suspensive veto (can delay, not stop, laws)."], [0, "Active citizens (men paying taxes = 3 days' wages) vote for electors; passive citizens can't vote. Only about 50,000 of 25 million qualify to be electors or legislators; women excluded."], [0, "Result: power shifts from noble birth to property."], [0, "Olympe de Gouges (1791): rewrites the Declaration to include women; demands equal property rights and recognition of fathers. \"Women, wake up.\""], [0, "Julien Raymond (1791): free people of color demand rights; granted, taken back, then confirmed in 1792."], [0, "Declaration's universal language gives excluded groups a standard to demand inclusion."], [0, "83 departments replace the old provinces; parlements and noble courts abolished; elected judges; torture and branding banned."], [0, "Metric system introduced (adopted 1795): rational, uniform measures."], [0, "Guilds abolished; free grain trade; peasants must pay to end leftover feudal dues."], [0, "Chapelier Law (1791): bans workers' associations → workers left to the free market."]]], ["Church Lands & Assignats (370)", [[0, "Assembly won't cancel the debt (owed to Third Estate bankers and merchants); new taxes fall short."], [0, "Solution: seize and sell Church land → inflation, religious split, civil war."], [0, "Assignats (Dec 1789): bonds backed by Church land; used as money; overprinted → inflation hurts the urban poor."]]], ["Civil Constitution of the Clergy (370-372)", [[0, "July 1790: Church becomes a branch of the state; bishoprics cut from 135 to 83 (one per department)."], [0, "Priests and bishops elected and paid by the state; most religious orders dissolved; Pope not consulted."], [0, "Clergy must swear an oath to it: only 7 bishops and under half of parish priests do; the rest = \"refractory.\""], [0, "February 1791: Pius VI condemns it and the Rights of Man → start of a long Catholic offensive against liberalism."], [0, "Catholics forced to choose between faith and revolution; the Assembly's biggest blunder."]]], ["Counterrevolution (372)", [[0, "Émigrés: over 16,000 nobles flee and plot from nearby countries, led by Artois."], [0, "Flight to Varennes (June 20, 1791): royal family flees in disguise, caught at Varennes, returned to Paris."], [0, "Assembly claims the king was \"abducted,\" but he's clearly the chief counterrevolutionary → constitutional monarchy in doubt."], [0, "Declaration of Pillnitz (August 27, 1791): Austria and Prussia promise to intervene to protect the royal family."]]], ["Main Chain to Remember", [[-1, "Louis fires Necker → Bastille stormed → Great Fear → August 4 ends privileges → Rights of Man → Women's March brings king to Paris → Constitution of 1791 + active/passive citizens → Church land seized + assignats → Civil Constitution of the Clergy splits Catholics → émigrés + Flight to Varennes → Pillnitz threatens war."]]]], "4.3": [["Setting", [[0, "1791-1794: the king can't be trusted, foreign monarchs threaten France, and Paris radicals gain power."], [0, "War with Europe makes everything more extreme: the monarchy falls, the king is executed, and the Reign of Terror begins."], [0, "Main story: Pillnitz → Legislative Assembly → war → monarchy overthrown → republic → king executed → Europe reacts → Terror."]]], ["Main Characters", [[0, "Louis XVI & Marie Antoinette → overthrown, then executed (1793)"], [0, "Jacobins → radical republican club, inspired by Rousseau"], [0, "Girondists → Jacobin faction that leads France into war"], [0, "The Mountain → most radical Jacobins; allied with the sans-culottes"], [0, "Sans-culottes → Paris shopkeepers and artisans; want price controls and direct democracy"], [0, "Pauline Léon → asks for women's right to bear arms"], [0, "Duke of Brunswick → Prussian commander who threatens Paris"], [0, "Dumouriez → Girondist general who wins at Valmy, then defects"], [0, "Edmund Burke → Reflections on the Revolution in France (1790)"], [0, "Thomas Paine → The Rights of Man (1791-92), defends the revolution"], [0, "Pitt the Younger → cracks down on reformers in Britain"], [0, "Catherine the Great & Kosciuszko → Russia crushes Poland; Kosciuszko leads the Polish resistance"], [0, "Lazare Carnot → organizes the levée en masse"], [0, "Robespierre → leads the Committee of Public Safety; \"Republic of Virtue\""]]], ["Pillnitz & the Legislative Assembly (372-373)", [[0, "Pillnitz: Austria and Prussia will act only if all major powers agree; Britain won't, so it's practically meaningless, but French radicals take it seriously."], [0, "National Constituent Assembly ends (Sept 1791); its members can't serve in the new Legislative Assembly (met Oct 1, 1791)."], [0, "New assembly faces three problems: Church resistance, the king's flight, and Pillnitz."], [0, "What changed: A brand-new, inexperienced assembly takes over at a dangerous moment."]]], ["Jacobins & the Road to War (373-374)", [[0, "Jacobins: best-organized club, with branches across France; want a republic; use Rousseau's ideas of equality, popular sovereignty, and civic virtue."], [0, "Girondists lead the Legislative Assembly: émigrés must return or lose property; refractory priests must accept the Civil Constitution or lose state pensions. Louis vetoes both."], [0, "April 20, 1792: France declares war on Austria (allied with Prussia), starting wars that last, with short breaks, until Waterloo (1815)."], [0, "Both sides want war: Girondists think it will protect the revolution from its enemies; the king hopes defeat will restore his power."], [0, "Pauline Léon and other women petition for the right to bear arms; some women do serve."], [0, "War goes badly; Brunswick Manifesto (July 1792) threatens to destroy Paris if the royal family is harmed → more support for the war and more distrust of the king."], [0, "What changed: France is at war with Europe, and the war radicalizes the revolution."]]], ["Fall of the Monarchy (374)", [[0, "Late July 1792: radical Paris Commune takes over the city government."], [0, "August 10, 1792: crowd invades the Tuileries; several hundred Swiss guards and many Parisians killed; royal family takes refuge in the Legislative Assembly, then is imprisoned."], [0, "September Massacres (early Sept 1792): about 1,200 people in Paris jails killed, some nobles and priests but mostly common criminals, wrongly assumed to be counterrevolutionaries."], [0, "Commune forces the Assembly to call a new Convention elected by universal male suffrage."], [0, "What changed: The constitutional monarchy is over, and the Paris crowd is now the most powerful force in politics."]]], ["The Convention, Sans-culottes & Jacobins (374-376)", [[0, "September 20, 1792: patriotic French army stops Prussia at Valmy."], [0, "September 21, 1792: Convention meets and declares France a republic."], [0, "Sans-culottes (\"without knee breeches\"): shopkeepers, artisans, wage earners hurt by food shortages and inflation; want price controls, hate aristocrats, want direct democracy. Main tools: Paris Commune and crowds."], [0, "Jacobins differ: want representative government and a free economy; hate privilege but not wealth."], [0, "The Mountain (radical Jacobins seated high up) works with the sans-culottes; Girondists don't."], [0, "Louis tried (Dec 1792), convicted by a large majority, sentenced to death by a smaller one; Girondists try to spare him. Executed January 21, 1793. Marie Antoinette executed October 1793; their son dies in prison (1795)."], [0, "1793: war on Britain and Holland (Feb), then Spain (March); France driven out of Belgium; Dumouriez defects; royalist Vendée revolt led by nobles and priests."], [0, "Girondists started the war but can't win it or crush enemies at home → the Mountain takes over."], [0, "What changed: France is a republic with no king, at war with most of Europe, and the radicals are in charge."]]], ["Europe Reacts (376-378)", [[0, "Burke, Reflections (1790): attacks rebuilding France on \"blind rationalism\" that ignores history; predicts turmoil, the king and queen's deaths, and military dictatorship → handbook of European conservatives."], [0, "Paine, Rights of Man (1791-92): defends the revolution; outsells Burke in England, but Burke has more long-term influence."], [0, "By 1792, European monarchs see revolutionary France as a threat and repress reform at home."], [0, "Britain: Pitt suppresses reformers (London Corresponding Society); mob attacks chemist Joseph Priestley; habeas corpus suspended (1794); limits on press and meetings."], [0, "Poland: 1791 constitution (hereditary monarchy, ends liberum veto, equality before the law, toleration) alarms Russia."], [0, "Russia invades (1792); Prussia promised to defend Poland but instead joins Russia in the Second Partition (1793). Moving Prussian troops east helps France win in fall 1792."], [0, "Kosciuszko (American Revolution veteran) leads a 1794 rebellion; crushed → Third Partition (1795) by Russia, Prussia, and Austria. Poland disappears."], [0, "What changed: Conservative backlash spreads across Europe, and Poland is wiped off the map."]]], ["War & the Reign of Terror (379-382)", [[0, "France invades Belgium (1792); Convention offers to help all peoples overthrow monarchs (Nov 1792) and opens the Scheldt River, breaking a British treaty."], [0, "First Coalition (by April 1793): Austria, Prussia, Britain, Spain, Sardinia, Holland."], [0, "Reign of Terror (summer 1793-July 1794): the state uses executions and violence against real or imagined enemies; thousands arrested from all classes, including former revolutionaries."], [0, "April 1793: Committee of Public Safety (near-dictatorial power) and Committee of General Security created."], [0, "June 1793: sans-culottes invade the Convention and force out the Girondists → Mountain in full control; new democratic constitution approved but never used."], [0, "Levée en masse (August 1793, organized by Carnot): young men fight, married men make weapons, women make tents and clothes, children make bandages, old men boost morale. 800,000-1 million drafted; first \"total war.\""], [0, "Sept 29, 1793: price ceilings set to satisfy the sans-culottes; revolutionary armies crush revolts in the provinces."], [0, "Republic of Virtue: based on Rousseau's general will; public good over private interest; renamed streets, no powdered wigs, banned \"unrepublican\" plays."], [0, "Robespierre: dominant by late 1793; opposed the war in 1792; still wears a powdered wig; says the people must be led \"by reason\" and enemies \"by terror.\""], [0, "What changed: The revolution now uses terror and total war to survive."]]], ["Main Chain to Remember", [[-1, "Pillnitz → Legislative Assembly → Girondists push war (1792) → war goes badly + Brunswick Manifesto → Aug 10 monarchy falls → September Massacres → Convention declares republic → Louis executed (1793) → First Coalition + Vendée revolt → Burke vs. Paine, repression in Britain, Poland partitioned → Mountain expels Girondists → Committee of Public Safety + levée en masse → Reign of Terror."]]]], "4.4": [["Setting", [[0, "1793-1799: the Reign of Terror peaks, turns on its own leaders, and ends with Robespierre's fall."], [0, "France swings back toward order: the Thermidorian Reaction and the Directory put property owners back in charge."], [0, "Main story: Republic of Virtue → women's clubs banned → de-Christianization → tribunals → Robespierre turns on revolutionaries → Thermidor → white terror → Directory → army gains power."]]], ["Main Characters", [[0, "Robespierre → leads the Terror; executed on 9 Thermidor (July 1794)"], [0, "Pauline Léon & Claire Lacombe → found the Society of Revolutionary Republican Women"], [0, "Olympe de Gouges → opposes the Terror; guillotined (Nov 1793)"], [0, "Enragés → extreme sans-culottes leaders; executed (March 1794)"], [0, "Jacques Danton → revolutionary hero; executed (April 1794)"], [0, "Thermidorians → moderates who end the Terror"], [0, "Napoleon Bonaparte → young general who crushes a royalist uprising (1795)"], [0, "Gracchus Babeuf → leads the Conspiracy of Equals (1796)"]]], ["Robespierre's Justification of Terror (382-383)", [[0, "Robespierre (early 1794): lead the people \"by reason\" and the people's enemies \"by terror.\""], [0, "In a revolution, government needs both: \"virtue, without which terror is fatal; terror, without which virtue is powerless.\""], [0, "Terror = \"prompt, severe, inflexible justice\" that comes from virtue."], [0, "Textbook: Robespierre was among the first secular ideologues who caused great suffering \"in the name of humanity.\""], [0, "Terror policies: excluding women from politics, de-Christianization, revolutionary tribunals."], [0, "What changed: Terror is now official policy, defended as a form of virtue."]]], ["Women Pushed Out of Politics (383)", [[0, "May 1793: Pauline Léon & Claire Lacombe found the Society of Revolutionary Republican Women to fight internal enemies."], [0, "Jacobins welcome it at first, but it grows radical: wants stricter price controls, hunts food hoarders, fights market women, demands the right to wear the cockade."], [0, "October 1793: Jacobins ban all women's clubs, using Rousseau's \"separate spheres\" (men = politics and army; women = home)."], [0, "Olympe de Gouges opposes the Terror and accuses Jacobins of corruption → guillotined (November 1793)."], [0, "Women also banned from the army and the Convention's galleries."], [0, "What changed: Women are shut out of public political life."]]], ["De-Christianization (383-384)", [[0, "November 1793: new revolutionary calendar starting from the Republic's first day (Sept 22, 1792); 12 months of 30 days; every 10th day a holiday instead of Sunday."], [0, "Month names linked to seasons (ex: Thermidor, Brumaire, Prairial)."], [0, "Notre Dame Cathedral declared a \"Temple of Reason.\""], [0, "Deputies on mission close churches, persecute Catholic and Protestant clergy, force some priests to marry, and kill some priests and nuns; churches used as barns."], [0, "Huge popular opposition turns provinces against Paris."], [0, "Robespierre opposes it, thinking it will cost the republic support."], [0, "What changed: The attack on religion turns much of rural France against the revolution."]]], ["Revolutionary Tribunals (384)", [[0, "Summer 1793: tribunals try \"enemies of the republic,\" a definition that keeps growing (foreign helpers → threats to virtue → good republicans who disagree)."], [0, "Guillotine: seen as efficient and humane (one quick cut)."], [0, "Provinces: mass shootings and drownings; at Nantes, hundreds tied to rafts and drowned in the Loire."], [0, "First victims (Oct 1793): Marie Antoinette and aristocrats, then Girondists."], [0, "Early 1794: thousands executed in the provinces, mostly peasants; eventually victims from every class, even sans-culottes."], [0, "What changed: The Terror spreads beyond nobles to anyone, rich or poor."]]], ["The Revolution Eats Its Own (384-385)", [[0, "March 24, 1794: Robespierre has the enragés (radical sans-culottes leaders) executed."], [0, "April 1794: Danton and his allies executed, accused of profiting from the revolution and not being radical enough."], [0, "Law of 22 Prairial (June 10, 1794): tribunals can convict without real evidence → executions rise."], [0, "May 1794: Robespierre replaces the Cult of Reason with the Cult of the Supreme Being, a deist civic religion based on Rousseau; Festival of the Supreme Being (June 8)."], [0, "What changed: Robespierre is attacking fellow revolutionaries, so no one feels safe."]]], ["Fall of Robespierre (385)", [[0, "July 26, 1794: Robespierre claims unnamed leaders are conspiring against him."], [0, "July 27, 1794 (9 Thermidor): Convention shouts him down; he's arrested."], [0, "July 28: he and about 80 supporters are executed without trial."], [0, "Sans-culottes don't save him: he killed their leaders and supported capping workers' wages."], [0, "Other Jacobins feared being his next victims."], [0, "Terror's toll: more than 25,000 victims."], [0, "What changed: The Terror ends, and the radical phase of the revolution is over."]]], ["Thermidorian Reaction (385-388)", [[0, "Thermidorian Reaction: backlash against radicalism after July 1794; people are tired of the Terror and fear the sans-culottes."], [0, "Girondists return; political prisoners freed; Committee of Public Safety weakened; Law of 22 Prairial repealed."], [0, "Paris Commune outlawed; Paris Jacobin Club closed (Nov 1794); Jacobin clubs banned from coordinating."], [0, "\"White terror\": former terrorists attacked and killed; \"bands of Jesus\" murder suspected Jacobins in Lyons, Toulon, Marseilles."], [0, "Wealthy middle class back in control; luxury, plays, and fancy dress return."], [0, "Catholic worship revives; refractory priests return."], [0, "Traditional gender roles reinforced; women have less freedom after 1795 than before 1789."], [0, "What changed: Power swings back to property owners, and the revolution turns conservative."]]], ["The Directory (388)", [[0, "Constitution of the Year III (1795): rejects both monarchy and democracy."], [0, "Two houses: Council of Elders (men over 40, married or widowed) and Council of Five Hundred (men over 30)."], [0, "Executive: 5-man Directory, chosen by the Elders from a list made by the Five Hundred."], [0, "Property requirements limit voting, but all soldiers can vote."], [0, "Lasting results: society based on property, not birth; representation is permanent; citizen soldiers show the power of nationhood."], [0, "Winners: middle class (commerce, professions, land) and especially peasants, who now own their land without paying anyone back."], [0, "What changed: France has a property-based republic, and the Old Regime is permanently gone."]]], ["Removing the Sans-culottes & Rise of the Army (388-390)", [[0, "Thermidorians end price ceilings → winter of 1794-95 has the worst food shortages; food riots crushed."], [0, "October 5, 1795 (13 Vendémiaire): royalist uprising in Paris; Napoleon breaks it with a \"whiff of grapeshot.\""], [0, "Treaties of Basel (1795): peace with Prussia and Spain; war with Austria and Britain continues."], [0, "Two-Thirds Law: two-thirds of the new legislature must come from the Convention → people lose faith in the new government."], [0, "1796: Babeuf's Conspiracy of Equals demands more democracy and equal property; Babeuf executed. Later socialists see him as a hero."], [0, "Directory faces too many problems (no sans-culottes support, narrow voting, Two-Thirds Law, royalist revival) → depends on the army, full of ambitious officers."], [0, "1799: Napoleon's coup (18 Brumaire) overthrows the Directory."], [0, "What changed: The army becomes the real power in France, setting up Napoleon."]]]], "5.1": [["Setting", [[0, "Europe, about 1400-1800. This act jumps across 4 chapters (Renaissance, Reformation, Scientific Revolution, Enlightenment) to follow one theme: women and family life."], [0, "Big picture: each movement talks about women, but almost none make them equal until Wollstonecraft at the end."]]], ["Main Characters", [[0, "Christine de Pisan = Renaissance \"first feminist,\" Treasure of the City of Ladies"], [0, "Luther & Calvin = married reformers who praise wives as partners"], [0, "Margaret Cavendish = English duchess, top woman in 1600s science, Royal Society critic"], [0, "Maria Winkelmann = German astronomer, rejected by the Berlin Academy bc of gender"], [0, "Emilie du Châtelet = helped Voltaire w/ Newton, translated the Principia"], [0, "Krämer & Sprenger = monks who wrote The Hammer of Witches (1486)"], [0, "Salon hostesses (Geoffrin, Lespinasse, Tencin) + Pompadour = backed the philosophes"], [0, "Rousseau = Émile (1762), separate spheres"], [0, "Mary Wollstonecraft = Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792)"]]], ["Christine de Pisan's Advice to Wives (67)", [[0, "Artisans' wives should work hard, learn the trade well enough to run the workers, and warn husbands about bad deals and too much credit."], [0, "Keep him happy so he stays home instead of wasting money in taverns (\"a quarrelsome wife, a smoking fireplace, a leaking roof\" drive a man out)."], [0, "Wife stays home too: no gossiping, no pointless pilgrimages."], [0, "What changed: a woman writes advice for women and treats wives as real partners in the family business, though still in a supporting role."]]], ["The Reformation & Women (126-127)", [[0, "Protestants push clerical marriage, reject celibacy and monasticism."], [0, "Drop the medieval Eve (temptress) vs. Mary (virgin) view; praise women as wives and mothers. Luther: men \"cannot do without women.\""], [0, "Companionate marriage = husband and wife as coworkers in a God-made family."], [0, "Divorce + remarriage allowed for adultery and abandonment (Catholics only allowed separation)."], [0, "Ex-nuns write exposés of convents, but some noblewomen protest closings bc convents gave them more independence."], [0, "Girls taught to read the Bible; some women find verses on equality before God and write for the Reformation."], [0, "What changed: marriage becomes sacred and women gain protection, literacy, and divorce, but stay under their husbands."]]], ["Family Life (127-130)", [[0, "Later marriages (1500-1800): men mid to late 20s, women early to mid 20s, bc couples needed time to afford living alone. Both churches now require parental consent + public vows."], [0, "Results: 1 in 5 women never marry, shorter marriages, more remarriage, more illegitimate kids."], [0, "Arranged marriages: parents negotiate, but couples usually knew each other and could legally refuse a forced match."], [0, "Family size: nuclear family; 6-7 births, 1/3 die by age 5, 1/2 by teens."], [0, "Birth control: weak, opposed by church (Aquinas: sex is for kids)."], [0, "Wet nursing: church + doctors condemn it (more infant deaths). Rich women use it for status; nobles want it so wives get pregnant faster (nursing ~75% effective as birth control)."], [0, "Loving families? Kids 8-13 sent to apprenticeships, quick remarriages. Looks cold, but preparing kids for a trade was seen as love."], [0, "What changed: family life is shaped by money and survival; marriage gets later and more regulated, but is still based on real affection."]]], ["Women & the Scientific Revolution (218-221)", [[0, "Women barred from universities, scientific societies, and court patronage (exception: Queen Christina brings Descartes to Sweden)."], [0, "Only ways in: noblewomen or artisan-class women, through husbands or male relatives."], [0, "Cavendish: argued w/ Descartes and Hobbes, mocked the Royal Society for loving instruments over useful problems (\"boys playing with bubbles\")."], [0, "Winkelmann: found a comet in 1702 (credited to her husband until 1930). After he died she applied to continue his work using guild tradition; Berlin Academy rejected her bc of gender."], [0, "Science books for women appear (Newtonianism for Ladies, 1737). Du Châtelet knew more math than Voltaire and made his Newton book possible."], [0, "What changed: by the early 1700s, science is assumed to be a male job, and new biology claims women's minds are inferior."]]], ["Witch Hunts: Why They Happened (227-229)", [[0, "1400-1700: an estimated 70,000-100,000 executed for harmful magic and devil worship (sabbats, flying, orgies w/ the devil)."], [0, "Peak = same years as the religious wars; division + war = panic."], [0, "Village roots: \"cunning folk\" helped w/ disasters; poor old single/widowed women claimed powers for status."], [0, "Clergy: church says its sacraments are God's power, so magic outside the church must come from the devil → \"pacts\" w/ Satan."], [0, "Princes: replacing local law w/ Roman law. Trials let church + state prove total authority over villages."], [0, "What changed: respected folk magic gets redefined as devil worship, and trials become a tool of church and state power."]]], ["Who Were the Witches & How It Ended (229-232)", [[0, "~80% women, mostly single, over 40. Misogyny is one theory; book says specific women were targeted for practical reasons."], [0, "3 targets: widows (alone, angry), midwives (blamed for deaths in childbirth), healers (magic w/o church approval)."], [0, "Hammer of Witches (1486), pope-approved guide: women are gullible, gossipy, carnal, defective (bent rib)."], [0, "Midwives: skilled trade but no guilds, licensed by men; baptized dying newborns, reported infanticide. Men take over in the 1700s w/ medical training and forceps."], [0, "End: (1) science separates mind and matter, so a curse is just words; (2) medicine, insurance, lawyers = more security; (3) accused start naming town leaders and judges."], [0, "What changed: hunts fade by 1700 bc science and security undercut the fear, and elites realize the trials could turn on them."]]], ["Women in the Enlightenment (333-334)", [[0, "Salon hostesses give philosophes contacts, status, and protection. Tencin promotes Montesquieu's Spirit of the Laws; Pompadour helps beat censorship of the Encyclopedia."], [0, "Philosophes want better education for women, not equality."], [0, "Montesquieu: uses the harem in Persian Letters to hint at women's repression; women not naturally inferior. But keeps traditional views of marriage and female chastity."], [0, "Encyclopedia: written by men; blames women's \"weakness\" on childbearing, motherhood = top job, double standard. Illustrations do show women working."], [0, "What changed: women spread the Enlightenment through salons, but its writers mostly keep women in traditional roles."]]], ["Rousseau vs. Wollstonecraft (334-337)", [[0, "Rousseau, Émile: separate spheres. Educate women to please men and raise kids; no politics or citizenship. Influences French Revolution leaders."], [0, "Still loved by women: made motherhood feel noble, got upper-class women to breast-feed."], [0, "Wollstonecraft, Vindication: men and women share the same reason, so they deserve the same education and virtue standards."], [0, "Separate spheres make women \"slaves\" w/ blind obedience; uneducated women hold back all of humanity."], [0, "What changed: Wollstonecraft applies the Enlightenment's own ideas of reason and liberty to women, adding women's rights to its agenda."]]], ["Main Chain to Remember", [[-1, "Christine de Pisan treats wives as working partners → Reformation makes marriage sacred, gives women literacy + divorce, but keeps them under husbands → family life shaped by late marriage and child death → women shut out of science except through husbands → witch hunts target old single women as church and state grab power, then fade → salon women spread the Enlightenment, but Rousseau locks them in separate spheres → Wollstonecraft uses reason to demand equality."], [-1, "\","], [-1, "\"viewUrl\": \"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Z1km5qXbd1Ug_74z_--P1NtZP-zQfJ883LpWY_tzLFU/edit?usp=drivesdk&ouid=100198233874477938101\","], [-1, "\"title\": \"Khare + Harris Euro Notes \""], [-1, "}"]]]]};
